          "textEn": "]7\n\nIn this physical world, from the first living being, Brahma, to a tiny ant, all\nliving beings are eager to obtain some kind of essence through their senses. These sensory pleasures\nare called rasa in a technical sense. There are many types of such rasas. In authentic scriptures, the names of the twelve\nrasas are enumerated as follows--(1) Raktad (Anger), (2) Adbhut (Wonder), (3) Shringar\n(Love), (4) Hasya (Joy), (5) Veera (Valor), (6) Daya (Compassion), (7) Dasya\n(Servitude), (8) Sakhya (Friendship), (9) Bhayanak (Fear), (10) Bibhatsa (Disgust),\n(19) Shant (Indifference), and (12) Vatsalya (Affection for parents).\n\nThe overall essence of all these rasas is affection or love. Basically, such characteristics of love, worship,\nservice, friendship, affection, and marital love, appear in the form of. When these five are absent,\nthen love indirectly appears in anger, surprise, laughter, bravery, fear, disgust, etc.\nFor example, if a man loves a woman, it is the genre of Shringar Rasa. But if such\nObstacles arise in love, it can lead to surprise, anger, shock, or even fear. Sometimes,\nThe end of the love relationship between two people occurs in brutal murder. Such Rasas are displayed between humans and\nbetween humans and animals. In this world, such exchange of essence\ndoes not happen between humans and animals, nor between humans and any other species. Such exchange of sap takes place only between organisms of the same species. But as far as souls\nare concerned, they are qualitatively similar to God. Therefore, in the beginning, the exchange of essence\nwas with the spiritual being (jivatma) and the spiritual supreme complete, God. This\nSpiritual exchange or essence; in the spiritual world, between beings and God, completely\nis found.\nTherefore, in the Shruti Sanhitas, the Supreme Personality of Godhead is referred to as \"Samasta Rasau Ka Nir Sakhot\". When a being associates with God and exchanges their natural essence with Him,\nthen they truly become happy."
