          "textEn": "49\n\nHe who is devoted to the supreme religion, who is free from the desire for worldly pleasures,\nHe who is free from the cause of unintentional offenses, he is pleased. 6>. Shabdartham\nSahah--He; Vai-Nishchayee--Certainly; Pansam--For humans; Parah--Divine; Dharmah Vritti; Yatah--From whom; Bhaktih--Devotion;\nAdhokshaje--Towards the self; Ahaitiki--Uncaused; Apratitah--Uninterrupted; Yaya--From whom; Atma--Soul;\nSuprasitati--Being pleased completely..\n\nThe ultimate tendency (dharma) for the entire humanity is that by which all humans can attain divine\ndevotion to God. Such devotion should be without cause and unbroken,\nso that the soul can be completely satisfied.\n\nMeaning: Through this statement, Shri Sut Goswami answers the first question of the sages of Naimisharanya. The sages requested Soot Ji to present the essence of all authentic scriptures,\nso that the fallen souls or the general public could easily grasp it. In the Vedas, for humans,\ntwo types of tendencies have been described.\nOne is called the path of nature or the path of sensory enjoyment, and\nthe other is called the path of renunciation or detachment. The path of enjoyment is inferior, and the path of cessation of the cause of the supreme cause is superior. The physical life of a living being is, in a way, a diseased state of real life.\nReal life is the state of being Brahman, that is, spiritual existence, where life is\neternal, blissful, and full of knowledge. Physical life is perishable, full of attachment and suffering. \"There is no comfort in this. Here, it is merely a futile attempt to get rid of suffering, and the momentary destruction of suffering is called false happiness. Therefore, the path of increasing material pleasures\nis inferior because it is perishable, painful, and full of attachment. But devotion to God is called the best religion,\nbecause it leads man towards a life full of eternal bliss and knowledge.\nIt leads. It sometimes gets contaminated when it has impurities.\nFor example,\ndevotional service done for material gain is an obstacle in the progressive retirement path. This patient\nRather than living life, renunciation or complete sacrifice is a superior state of mind. Such indulgence only leads to disease"
