          "textEn": "58\n\nMeaning of the word:\nThat-that; With devotion:--serious inquirer; Sages:--group of sages; Knowledge:--knowledge; Detachment:--detachment; With reason:--with reason;\nThey see:--seeing; In oneself:--in oneself; And:--and; The self:--to the Supreme Soul; With devotion:--with devotion; Heard:--from the Vedas;\nHaving grasped:--grasped well..\n\nA profound seeker or sage, endowed with knowledge and detachment, attains the supreme truth through the listening to the Vedas and Shrutis.\nThrough devotion, he experiences the supreme truth. Meaning: Lord Vasudeva, who is like the complete expression of the ultimate truth, through his devotional service,\nthe process of service leads to the realization of the ultimate truth. Brahma is their divine physical radiance, and\nthe Supreme Being is their partial form. Therefore, the experience of Brahma or the Supreme Being is merely a partial\nexperience of the ultimate truth. Humans are of four types: Karmi, Gyani, Yogi and Bhakta. The workers\nare materialistic, but the remaining three are spiritual. The devotees are first-class spiritualists\nwho have realized the Supreme Being.\nIn the second category come those who are spiritual,\nwho have partially realized the complete part of the Supreme Being, and in the third category come those\nwho have only a glimpse of the divine glimpse of the Supreme Being. As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita and other\nVedic texts, the realization of the Supreme Being is attained through devotional service, after which\ncomplete knowledge and detachment from material associations remain inherent. We have already told that\nAfter devotional service, detachment comes from knowledge and material association. Because Brahma and the Supreme Soul\nExperiences are incomplete experiences of that ultimate truth, therefore, to attain Brahma and the Supreme Soul,\nthe means, that is, knowledge and the path of yoga, are also incomplete means of experiencing that ultimate truth.\nDevotion is\nthe only complete method by which a serious seeker can experience the ultimate truth, because\nthe foundation of devotion stands on complete knowledge and detachment from material associations, and it is strengthened by the\nhearing of Vedanta-Shruti. Therefore, it is not that devotion is for the sake of the few spiritualists\nis. Devotees are of three categories--first, second and third. The third class devotee or newly initiated\ndevotee, who are without knowledge, they are not detached from material association. They are attracted to the worship of the deity in the temple,\nto the initial process of worship. Such a devotee is called a bhautik bhakta (devotee of material things)."
