          "textEn": "87\nThe constituents, proton, neutron, etc., are the various results of the supreme form of God. Just as\nfire manifests from wood or butter comes out of milk, similarly, in the form of the Supreme Being, God\nthe presence of whom can be experienced through the valid listening and chanting of those divine subjects,\nwhich are described in particular in Vedic literature such as the Upanishads and Vedanta. The Shrimad Bhagavatam is an authoritative interpretation of such Vedic literature. The realization of God can be achieved through the hearing of divine messages,\nand this is the only way to experience the divine subject. Just as fire manifests from wood when burned by another fire, similarly, the divine consciousness of man can be illuminated only by other divine compassion.\nShri Shrimad Pujya Guru Kasht\nBy the ears who listen humbly in the form of the soul, by inserting the appropriate divine message,\ncan ignite the spiritual fire. Therefore, with the desire to listen humbly,\none should go to the appropriate Guru. In this way, gradually, one experiences divine existence. The distinction between animism and humanity is based on this very method. Humans can hear properly, while\nanimals cannot do that. Those who are endowed with virtues, who are subtle and self-controlled,\nWho are independent and self-created, who are delighted in the beings, they are endowed with those virtues. 33. Shabdartham\nAasau--He is the Supreme Being; Gunamayah--full of qualities; Bhavai--Naturally; Bhutah--produced; Sukshma-sukshma;\nIndriya-indriyas; Atmabhimahi--by the soul; Swanirmiteshu--in one's own creation; Fi-fi--by entering; Bhunke--by enjoying;\nFor the sake of Bhuteshu--in living beings; Tat-gunan--those qualities of nature \\.\n\nThe Supreme Being enters the bodies of living beings influenced by the three qualities of the material world\nAnd through the subtle mind, they inspire beings to experience the fruits of the three Gunas. Meaning: From the mos