          "textEn": "88\nIt happens and the senses are produced according to the desire of the living being. God, in the form of the Supreme Soul, helps the living being\nto attain material happiness, because the living being is completely incapable of attaining any\nmaterial happiness according to its own will. He is the one who desires, and God is the one who regulates him. The intention is that all beings are parts of God, therefore they are distinct from God. In the Bhagavad-gita, the Lord calls various embodied beings His sons. On the happiness and sorrow of sons,\nParents also experience happiness and sorrow indirectly; even then, the father\nis not directly affected by the happiness and sorrow of the sons. They are so compassionate that they remain constantly in the form of God with the living being,\nand keep inspiring the living being towards true happiness.\nHe is the cause of the worlds being sustained by goodness, and He is the enjoyer of the worlds.\nHe is the cause of the incarnations of the gods, the demons, and the other beings. 34). Shabdartham\nBhavayati--they nurture; Eshah--of all these; Sattaven--from the quality of truth; Lokan--in the universe; Vai--in general;\nLokabhavanah--the master of all TRUE; Leela--activity; Avatar--incarnation; Anuratah--having assumed a role; Dev--deity; Tiryak--lower animal; Nar-Adishu--in humans..\n\nThus, the lords of all universes, the gods, humans, and lower animals, are inhabited.\nThey are the ones who nurture all the planets. They incarnate and perform divine plays for the salvation of those who live in pure Sattva Guna. Meaning: The physical universe is countless, and in each universe, there are countless planets, in which\ndifferent types of creatures of various qualities reside. Lord (Vishnu) among these each\nIn the world and in every living being, he keeps descending. He displays his divine plays among them,\nSo that they may have the interest to return to the abode of God.\nGod\ndoes not change his original divine state, but according to the country, time, and society, they appear in different\ndifferent forms."
