          "textEn": "25\n\nWhere the self-realized form is prohibited in the self-realized state,\nIt is known by the self-realized state that it is done in ignorance. 33). Meaning of the word\nWhere-also; these--in this; true-untrue; gross and subtle; in forms--in forms; prohibited--once removed; self-\nContracted--through self-realization; ignorance--from ignorance; in the self--in the self; done--attached; thus--\nThat is it; the vision of Brahman--the method of seeing God\n\nWhenever a person, through self-realization, experiences that gross and subtle\nboth bodies have no concern with the pure soul, at that time he experiences his own and also\nGod. Meaning: The difference between self-realization and delusion is that the temporary or illusory attachment of physical energy in the form of gross and subtle bodies is the external covering of the soul. These coverings are caused by ignorance. Such coverings are never effective on the personality of God. To know this properly is liberation or the vision of God.\nIt means that by adopting a divine-like\nThat is, by embracing a spiritual life, complete self-realization becomes possible. Self-\nThe meaning of self-realization is to be unconscious of the needs of the gross and subtle body, and\nTo be serious about the functions of the soul. Inspiration for actions originates from the soul, but\nbecause the soul does not know the true state, such actions become deceptive. Due to ignorance,\ncalculation of one's own benefit is done by keeping gross and subtle bodies in mind, as a result of which all\nactions, the entire structure of actions from birth to birth, gets disrupted. But when a person, through proper\npractice, meets the soul, then the beginning of the soul's functions takes place. As a result,\nthe person who remains engaged in the functions of the soul, even while living a bound life, * is called a 'Jivanmukta' (liberated while living).\nThis complete state of self-realization is not attained by any artificial