          "textEn": "88\n\nNa vai jano jatu kathancha-nravaje-\nNmukundasevyanvyadanga sansrutim.\nTFSI puna-\nVihatumichhenran rasagro janah. 19. Shabdartham\nNa--never; Vai--certainly; Janaah--person; Jatu-at some time; Kathancha-in some way; Atrajet-do not come;\nMukundaseevi-devotee of Lord; Anyavat-like others; Ank-this; Sansrithim-physical world; Smaran-remembering\nWhile doing; Mukundaadhri-feet of Lord; Upagrahanam-wandering; Punah-again; Vihatum-desire to abandon;\nFrom; Ichhet-desire; Na-never; Rasa-grasher-one who has tasted the essence; Janaah-person; ^. Hey dear Vyas, even though a devotee of Lord Krishna sometimes, for some reason or another,\nfalls down, but he does not have to come into the cycle of life like others (desire-driven beings, etc.)\nbecause the person who has tasted the lotus feet of God once,\ndoes not have to do anything other than remembering that bliss again and again.\nMeaning: A devotee of God becomes detached from the fascination of the world, because he is a receptor of essence,\nthat is, he has already tasted the lotus feet of God. Indeed, there are many such\nexamples in which devotees of God have fallen due to bad company, as if they are self-willed workers\nwhose downfall is always happening. But even after falling down, a devotee should never be considered as a worker.\nA worker experiences the fruits of his actions, while a devotee, through God,\nimproves due to being tormented. The suffering of an orphaned child and a prince are not the same. An orphan is truly poor, because there is no one to take care of him,\nBut a rich man's beloved son, even if he seems like an orphan, he always lives under the protection of his capable\nfather. A devotee of God sometimes imitates a desire-driven worker due to bad company.\nHe does this.\nA desireful worker wants to assert his dominance over the material world. Similarly, a novice\ndevotee, out of foolishness, thinks of accumulating some material power in exchange for devotional service."
