          "textEn": "89\nSometimes God puts such foolish devotees in trouble, ©. And if they bestow special grace,\nthen they take away all his material belongings. From such action, the worried devotee's\nall friends and relatives leave him and by the grace of God, he regains consciousness\nand he starts doing his devotion in the right way. In the Bhagavad Gita, it is also said that such fallen devotees are given the opportunity to be born in the lineage of a very learned Brahmin\nor in a prosperous Vaishya family. In such a situation, a devotee is not as fortunate as a devotee who is tormented by God, who is, in a way, placed in a helpless state. The devotee who becomes helpless by the will of God, he is more fortunate than the devotees born in these excellent families.\nThese fallen devotees, born in a noble family, may forget the lotus feet of God due to being less fortunate, but the helpless devotee is more fortunate, because he, finding himself helpless from all sides, quickly returns to the lotus feet of God. Pure devotion-filled service is so delicious from a spiritual point of view that the devotee automatically becomes unconscious of material pleasures. It symbolises completeness in the direction of progressive devotion. Pure\nA devotee constantly remembers the lotus feet of Lord Krishna and does not forget them even for a moment, even if he gets the enjoyment of all three worlds. This world is different from God.\nBecause the world is the cause of restlessness.\nThat itself is the Veda, yet it is also you.\nOnly a part of the region is displayed to you. 20>. Meaning of the word:\nIdam- this; Hi- complete; Vishcham- world; Bhagavan- God; Iva- usually like that; Itharah- others; Yat- from; Jagat- world; Sthana- place; Nirodh- destruction; Sambhavah- origin; Tat- that; Vijnanah- knower; Bhavan- you; Tathapi- yet; Tev- to you; Pradesha- only; Matra- only; Saransh- summary; Bhagavata- to you;\nPradashitam- told \\."
