          "textEn": "209\n\nAnd all actions of beings are for the destruction of the self.\nThey are imagined for the destruction of the self, imagined by the supreme. 34). Meaning of the word\nEvam-in this way; Nram-of the humans; Kriya-yoga-all activities; Sarve-all; Sansruti-physical existence;\nHetavah-causes; Eva-certainly; Atma-self; Kayam-like tree; Vinashaya-for destruction; Kalyante-capable;\nare; Kalyitah-dedicated; Pare-to God..\n\nIn this way, when all the activities of a human being are dedicated to the service of God, then\nthose same actions that are due to his immediate bondage, become the destroyers of the tree of action. Meaning: In the Bhagavad Gita, the desire-driven actions that keep living beings constantly busy are compared to a banyan tree,\nbecause its roots are very deep. As long as there is a desire to experience the fruits of karma,\nthe departure of the soul according to karma, from one body to another,\nfrom one place to another, keeps happening. This very desire for enjoyment can be transformed into the desire to serve God.\nBy doing this, a person's work changes into Karma Yoga or that path,\nso that a person, while performing actions according to their natural instinct, can attain spiritual completeness. Here, the word 'atma' (soul) indicates all the categories of karmic actions. The conclusion is that\nit comes out that when all desires and other actions are put into the service of God,\nthen new actions do not arise, and gradually divine devotional service develops,\nwhich will not only completely uproot the tree of karma, but it will take the doer\nto the lotus feet of God. The summary is that a person must first seek the company of such pure devotees who are not only\nknowledgeable in Vedanta, but also self-realized and unique devotees of Lord Shri Krishna. In this\ncontext, newly initiated devotees should serve with love, body and mind, without discrimination.\nInspired by this service, great men will be more eager to bestow their grace,\nso that the newly initiated devotee can be imbued with the divine qualities of pure devotees. Slowly, slowly from this"
