 अंश यह है कि मनुष्य को चाहिए कि भगवान्‌ के विभिन्न",
          "textEn": "204\n\nMeaning of the word:\nOm, a symbol of the divine glory of God; Namah--bowing to God; Bhagwate--to God;\nTulyam--to you; Vasudevaya--to the son of Vasudeva, God; Dheemahi--to pronounce or to sing; Pradyumna, Aniruddha\nSankarshana--these three are complete parts of Vasudeva; TH:—Respectful salutations; Cha--and..\n\nCome, let us all praise Vasudeva and his complete parts, including Pradyumna, Aniruddha, and Sankarshana.\nLet us sing their glory.\n\nMeaning: According to the Panchratra, Narayan is the original cause of all the expansions of God. This\nis the expansion of Vasudev, Sankarshan, Pradyumna, and Aniruddha. Vasudev and Sankarshan are seated to the left,\nPradyumna is to the right of Sankarshan, and Aniruddha is seated to the left of Vasudev. In this way, these four\ndeities remain seated. These four are known as Chaturvyuha of Lord Krishna. This is a Vedic mantra that begins with the symbol A-kara-Pranava.\nThus, this mantra is established in the form of 3%\nNamo Dheemahi... through divine utterance (kirtan). The meaning is that any exchange, whether it is in the realm of karmic actions or in the realm of knowledge,\nwhose ultimate goal is not the divine realization of God, is considered futile. Therefore, Narad, based on his experience, explains the nature of unique devotional service,\nin which the inner relationship between God and the individual is developed through the gradual method of progressive devotional activities.\nFor God, such progress in divine devotion is the end of God\nin loving service, which is called love, and which is called rasa in its divine diversities. Such devotion is also accomplished in mixed forms, such as with desire-driven actions or arguments.\nNow, the question that Shaunaka and other sages raised, and which was related to the secret aspects of Soot Ji's achievement through the guru, its explanation has been given in this verse, and that is\nthe chanting of this thirty-three-letter mantra. This mantra addresses four Vibhagas, or God, along with their\ncompleteness. The central Vibhaga is Lord Krishna, because these complete parts are their\nguardians. The most secret part of this teaching is that man should have the various of God"
