          "textEn": "248\n\nOh, this is the wealth of gods, the fame of which is greater than the sky.\nHe who sings of this, the first, is delighted by the sound of the string. 38). Meaning of the word:\nAho--Blessed; Devarshi--The sage of the gods; Dhanyah--Blessed; Ayam--Who; Kirtim--Praise; Shar--Shar\nDhanvanah--Of the Lord; Gayan--Singing; Madhyan-Enjoying bliss; Idam--This; Tanvya--With the help of the instrument;\nRamayati--Gives life; Aturam-In distress; Jagat-The world;\n\nDevarshi Narad is blessed, because he sings the praises of the Lord's pastimes, and by doing so, he himself enjoys bliss, and also inspires all the suffering beings in the universe. Meaning: Shri Narad Muni plays his Veena to sing the praises of the divine plays of God and\nto support all the suffering beings in the universe. In this universe, no one\nis happy, and what we experience as happiness is the illusion of Maya.\nGod's illusion is so strong that even a carnivorous pig feels happy.\nIn this physical world, no one can be truly happy. Srila Narada Muni, to the sorrowful residents,\nkeep wandering everywhere to provide ANION. Their only purpose is to take them back to the abode of God. This is the purpose of all those true devotees of God, who walk on the footprints of this\nMaharishi.\n\nThus, within the first book of Shriyadbhagavat, the Bhakti Vedanta Tatparya is completed in the sixth chapter named \"Dialogue between Narada and Vyasadeva\"."
