          "textEn": "288\n\nAthopetya Swashibramam Govindapriyasarathi.\nNyavedatam Priyayai Shonchyaatma Janan Hatahan. 41). Meaning of the word:\nAth--thereafter; Upetya--having reached; Swa--own; Shibiram--in the camp; Govinda--one who makes the senses active;\n(Lord Krishna); Priya-beloved; Sarathi-the charioteer; Nyavedayat-provided; Tam-to him; Priyayai-beloved;\nShochantyai-lamenting; Atma-jan-for one's own sons; Hata-killed..\n\nArjun, along with his beloved friend and charioteer (Shrikrishna), reached his camp and handed over that\nkiller to his beloved wife, who was lamenting for her dead sons. Note: Arjuna's divine relationship with Krishna is in the form of a close friendship. Bhagavad Gita.\nGod himself has called Arjun his dearest friend. In this way, every living being, in some way or another,\nis connected to God through a dear relationship, whether it is of a servant or of a friend, or of parents,\nor of sweet love.\nThrough the method of Bhakti Yoga, every person can attain the presence of God in the spiritual world if they have such a desire and make sincere efforts for it. And thus, like an animal, bound by its own nature,\nDesirous of pleasures, averse to actions,\nBeing watched, Krishna, having pleased the Guru,\nWith a gentle demeanor, he bows down. 49>. Meaning of the word\nTatha--in this way; ahatam--taken; pashu-vat--like an animal; pasha-bandham--tied with ropes; avak-mukham--without a single word coming out of the mouth; karma-karya--action; jugupsiten--being disgusting; nirikch-seeing: Krishna--\nDraupadi; apakrutam--one who does unrighteous deeds; gurouh-of the guru; sutam-to the son; vam--beautiful; swabhava-of one's own nature;\nKripya-out of pity; naman-bowed; cha-therefore; tatha..\n\nShri Sut Goswami said: Then Draupadi saw Ashwatthama, who was like an animal,\nhad been impaled with ropes, and because of committing a very heinous act, silently AT. His wife"
