          "textEn": "38\nThey remained, but even though they were the king's son in the Krishna incarnation, they renounced the protection of their real parents (King\nVasudeva and Queen Devaki) at birth and went to Yashoda Mai's lap.\nBecause of his childhood pastimes, in the sacred land of Vrindavan, he\nwas able to act like an ordinary cowherd, therefore Lord Krishna is more merciful than Lord Rama. He was especially kind to Kunti's brother Vasudeva and his family. If Krishna had not taken birth as the son of Vasudeva and Devaki, then Queen Kunti would not have been able to address him with so much affection, considering him her nephew. But the more fortunate are\nNanda and Yashoda, who have tasted the essence of the childhood pastimes of the Lord, which are more captivating than other pastimes.\nThere is nothing else like his childhood pastimes displayed in Vrajbhumi Vrindavan.\nIt is a sample of his eternal pastimes happening in the original Krishnaloka described in the Brahma Samhita by the name of Chintamani Dham.\nLord Shri Krishna himself descended in Vrajbhumi with his divine associates and\nmaterials. Therefore, Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu has confirmed that\nno one is as fortunate as the residents of Vrajbhumi, and among them, the Gopis are especially\nfortunate, because they sacrificed everything for the pleasure of God. He, along with Nanda and Yashoda, along with the cowherds, and especially along with the cowherds and cows,\nwas called Govinda because of his divine plays. In the form of Govinda, God is especially kind to Brahmins and cows.\nWhich means that human prosperity depends a lot on these two things--one is Brahmin culture and the other is cow protection. Where there is a lack of both of these, there God\nKrishna is never pleased.\nSalutations to the lotus-eyed one, salutations to the one adorned with lotus garlands.\nSalutations to the lotus-eyed one, salutations to the lotus-bearing one. 22)."
