          "textEn": "330\n\nMeaning of the word:\nN--No; Ved--Knower; Kashchit--Anyone; Bhagavan--Lord; Chikirshitam--Leela, Tava--Yours; Ihmanasya--Like worldly people; N--Never; Yasya--Whose;\nKashchit--Anyone; Dayitah--Special favor-worthy; Asti--Is; Kartiti--Done; Dveshyah--Object of hatred; Ch--And;\nYasmin--In which; Vishama--Different; Mati--Thought; Nrammana--Of humans..\n\nOh God, no one can understand your divine plays, because they appear human.\nAnd therefore, they are misleading. Neither is anyone particularly worthy of your grace, nor\nis anyone unpleasant to you. It is only people's imagination that you are punishing. Meaning: God's grace is distributed equally to the fallen souls. They do not keep enmity with anyone. The very concept of understanding God as a human is misleading. His divine plays appear to be in the image of a human, but in reality, they are divine and completely free from any material impurity.\nUndoubtedly, they are considered to be biased towards their pure devotees, but\nin reality, they never discriminate, just as the sun does not discriminate against anyone. From the rays of the sun, sometimes even a stone becomes precious, whereas an unknowing person, even after living in abundant sunlight, cannot see. Darkness and light are two opposite concepts, but it does not mean that the sun is biased in distributing its rays. Sun rays\nare available to everyone, but the capabilities of the receivers are different. Foolish people think that\ndevotion is to gain special grace from God by flattering him. In fact, God,\nThe pure devotees engaged in divine loving service are not a community of merchants. The merchant class serves in the exchange of wealth.\nBut a pure devotee does not serve for such transactions. Therefore, the door of God's\ngrace is always open for him.\nDistressed and needy people, curious or\nintellectual people establish temporary relationships with God for some benefit. But when their work is accomplished, they have no connection with God. Any\nsad person, if even slightly pure, prays to God for salvation. But as soon as his sufferings are removed, often such a distressed person, beyond God, his own"
