          "textEn": "349\nAlso indicate changes in functions. Mrs. Kunti, without being disturbed, wanted to serve God.\nAnd this was her prayer. This exclusive devotion (Nishtha) is the ultimate goal of life. Generally, our attention gets diverted towards the service of such an object,\nwhich is beyond God or is not in God's plan. When this plan turns into the service of God,\nthat is, when the senses are purified by the service of God, then this\nservice is called pure exclusive devotion. Mrs. Kuntidevi had this very completeness in her wish, and she\nwas praying to God for this very thing. Their affection towards the Pandavas and Vrishnis is not beyond the scope of devotion, because\nserving God and serving devotees are inseparable. Sometimes, the service of a devotee is\ngreater than the service of God. But here, Kunti Devi's affection towards the Pandavas and Vrishnis\nwas due to family relations.\nIn the context of physical relationships, this bond of affection is a relationship of illusion,\nbecause the relationships of body or mind are due to the influence of external energy.\nWhen established towards the Supreme Being, the relationships of the soul are true relationships. Kunti Devi\nBy severing family ties, the intention was that they wanted to sever the blood relations.\nThis blood relation is the cause of worldly bondage, but the soul's relationship is the cause of freedom. This relationship of soul to soul can be established through the relationship with the Supreme Soul. Seeing in the dark is not seeing at all. But to see in the sunlight means\nto see the sun and besides that, to see everything that remained unseen in the darkness. This is the path of Bhakti Yoga. Shrikrishna, Krishna's friend, the ocean of Vrishnu's essence,\nThe destroyer of the dynasty of kings, the best of the best, the mighty. Govinda, the destroyer of the sufferings of the gods, the incarnation of the destroyer,\nLord of all the gurus, the lord of the yogis, I bow to you. 42)."
