          "textEn": "354\n\nThe Brahmins, by their own nature, have fallen into the delusion of attachment and delusion. 47). Shabdartham\nAah-- said; Raja-- King Yudhishthira; Dharma-Suta-- son of Dharma (Yama); Chintayant- thinking; Suhadam- of friends;\nVadham- killing; Prakriten- by physical thought; Atmanah- by oneself; fawn:-- O Brahmins; Sneha- affection; Moha- delusion;\nVam- subjugated by; Gatah- gone..\n\nDharmaputra, King Yudhishthira, was overwhelmed by the death of his friends and was grief-stricken like a common\nmaterialistic man. Oh sages, thus, being deluded by affection,\nthey spoke.\nMeaning: Although it was not expected that Maharaj Yudhishthira would become as grief-stricken as an ordinary human, but as per Lord Kaush's wish, he became deluded due to worldly affection.\nWhich, as per Arjun, seemed to be deluded. The person who understands, knows very well\nthat life is the body, not the mind, but life is beyond the physical concept. Ordinary\nman sees violence and non-violence in relation to the body, but this is a kind of delusion. Every person is bound by duty according to their nature. A Kshatriya has to fight for the right cause,\nwithout caring about the opposing side.\nThus, while performing their duty,\na person should not be distracted by the destruction of the body, because this body is the external attire of a living soul.\nAll this was known to Maharaj Yudhishthira, but by the will of God, he\nbecame like an ordinary man, because behind this attachment there was another very great feeling that\nthe king Bhishma should give the same kind of advice as Lord himself had given to Arjuna. Oh, I have seen the knowledge of the self, firmly established in the wicked.\nFor the body of the wicked, many are the sacrifices of my eyes. 48. Meaning of the word:\nAho--Hi; Me--Mine; Pashyat--Just look; Ajnanam--Ignorance; Hadi--In the heart; Rudram--Standing; Duratmanah--Of the sinner;\nParakyasya--For others; Ev--Indeed; Dehasya--Of the body; Bahyah-