          "textEn": "405\n\nIn the battlefield (where Lord Krishna was staying with Arjuna in friendship)\nLord Krishna was stained with the dust raised by the tapping of his waving hair horses, and due to the labor, his\nface was soaked with drops of sweat. From the wounds made by my sharp arrows, these\nornaments were looking good to them. My mind went to those same Shri Krishna. Meaning: God is the embodiment of truth, knowledge and bliss. Therefore, when the divine\nloving service of God is done with five main relationships (raso), that is, peace, servitude, friendship, affection, and goodness,\nif it is done with real love and affection in any one of them, then God accepts it.\nShri\nBhishmadev, a great devotee of God, with a sense of servitude. Therefore, the sharp\nrain of arrows on the divine body of God is like the rain of tender rose flowers on them by another devotee.\nIs. It seems that Bhishmadev, along with God, is repenting for whatever he had done.\nBut in reality, because of his divine body, God did not feel even a little\npain. His body is not matter. He himself and his body are both completely\nspiritual. The soul cannot be pierced, nor burned, dried, or wetted.\nIt can be. Its detailed explanation is given in the Bhagavad Gita. A similar statement is also found in the Skanda Purana. It is said in it that the soul is always without sin and imperishable. Neither can it be given pain,\nnor can it be dried up. When Lord Vishnu incarnates and appears before us,\nhe appears bound by material bonds like bound souls, but he\ndoes so to enchant the demons or the unbelievers who are always in the pursuit of killing God from the beginning of his incarnation. Kansa wanted to kill Krishna and Ravana wanted to kill Ram.\nThey were, because out of foolishness, they were unaware of the fact that God can never be killed,\nbecause the soul is never destroyed."
