त बद्धजीवों के कल्याण के",
          "textEn": "409\nWhen Arjuna, in the battlefield, was seeing the soldiers and commanders before him and seemed to be getting tainted by ignorance,\nGod, by bestowing divine knowledge on his ignorance,\ncompletely destroyed it. May his lotus feet always remain the object of my attraction. Meaning: Kings and commanders had to stand before the fighting soldiers. This is the system of real warfare. Then kings and commanders were not like today's so-called presidents or defense ministers. When poor soldiers or mercenary soldiers were fighting face to face with each other,\nthe king or commander could not sit at home. This can be the law of modern democracy, but when there was a real monarchy, the emperors were not like those who were cowards to be chosen without merit.\nAs is clear from the battlefield of Kurukshetra,\nthe executive heads of both sides, namely Drona, Bhishma, Arjuna, and Duryodhana, were not sleeping; they were all\nparticipating in such a war that was going to take place far away from public accommodation places. This means that innocent citizens were saved from the effects of the war between rival royal parties.\nThey were. In such a war, whatever was happening, there was no need for it to be seen by the citizens. They had to give one-fourth of their income to the ruler, whether it was Arjuna or\nDuryodhana. In the battle of Kurukshetra, the commanders of both armies stood face to face. Arjun looked at them\nwith extreme compassion and began to regret that due to the greed for power, he would kill his own relatives.\nHe was not at all afraid of the terrible military formation created by Duryodhana,\nbut being a kind devotee of God, it was natural that he would detach himself from worldly things,\ntherefore he decided that he would not fight for worldly glory. But this was due to little knowledge; that is why it is said that his intellect was tainted.\nOff. His intellect should never have been tainted, because he was a devotee of God and\na friend, as is clear from the fourth chapter of the Bhagavad Gita. Certainly, Arjuna's intellect was tainted.\nOtherwise, through the intellect of the body, for the welfare of the tainted bound souls trapped in material bondage"
