          "textEn": "448\nGo, that is Shrutisanstra. The Acharya Thakur Narottamdas of the Gaudiya Sampradaya has written many songs in simple Bengali language. But a very different scholar Acharya Thakur Vishwanath of the same sect\nChakravarti has described these songs of Narottamdas as similar to Vedic mantras. The reason for this is\ntheir subject matter. Language is not as important as the subject matter. All those women\nwho were absorbed in the thoughts and actions of God, by the grace of God, the Vedic literature\nhad developed consciousness. Therefore, even though they were not good scholars of Sanskrit,\neven then, their words seemed more beautiful than the Vedic hymns.\nIn the Upanishads, Vedic\nhymns are sometimes indirectly expressed towards God. But the words of women were directly\nabout God, therefore they were more heart-touching. The words of women seemed more valuable than the blessings of learned Brahmins.\nThat man is the fortress: ancient\nyet a special soul. In the beginning, in the self, in the world, in the self, in the self, in the self, in the self, in the self. 21). Shabdartham\nSah-He (Krishna); Vai-As I remember; Kil-Certainly; Ayam-This; Purushah-God; Puranah-Old;\nYah-He; Ekah-One; Asit-Was; Avishishtah-Unattached; Avishishtah-Unattached; Aatmani-Self; Agra-Before; Srishti-Creation;\nGunebhyah-Of the qualities; Jagat-In the world; Atmani-In the self; Nimilit-Into; Atman-Self;\nJiva-Living being; Nishi Suptah-Asleep at night; Shaktis-Of the powers..\nShe began to say: As we certainly remember, these are they, who are the first God.\nBefore the manifest creation of the qualities of nature, they alone existed. Because they are God,\ntherefore all beings are absorbed in them, as if they are sleeping at night and their power has stopped\n. Meaning: This visible world is merged in two ways. After every 4,32,00,00,000 solar years,\nwhen the lord of a universe, Brahma, goes to sleep, there is a deluge. Brahma"
