          "textEn": "284\n\nMeaning of the word:\nNa- not; Jatu- at any time; Kaurav- of the Kuru dynasty; Om- of the king Om; Dordand- by strength; Parirambhite- protected; Gaya; Bhutale- on earth; Anupanti- while grieving; Asmin- until now; Vina- without; Te- yours; Praninam- living beings; Ka- of; Shuchah- with eyes; Ashru- tears.\n\nIn the kingdom protected by the strength of the Kuru dynasty's king Om, today I am seeing you for the first time in my eyes\nwith tears filled and grieving. Until today, no one has shed tears on the face of the earth for the neglect of a king.\nBecause of that. Meaning: Protecting the lives of humans and animals is the government's first and most important duty. No government should discriminate in such rules. In this Kali Yuga, for any pure\nhearted person, seeing such a well-planned art of creatures by the state is terrifying. King Parikshit was grieving seeing the tears in the eyes of the bull and was surprised to see this.\nHe was wondering why such an unprecedented event was happening in his excellent rule.\nAs far as life is concerned,\nhumans and animals were equally protected. This is the way of the kingdom of heaven. Oh, beautiful one, may you be pure and free from the fear of the Vrishala. O destroyer of the wicked, I am your refuge. 9>. Meaning of the word:\nMa--mother; Saurabheya--son of Saurabha; Atra--in my kingdom; Shuchiha--sorrow; Vyetu--let it be; Te--yours; Vrushalat--by Shra;\nBhayam--the cause of fear; Ma--believed; Rodi--to cry; Amb--mother of a cow; HEH—well-being; Te-yours; Khalanaam--inmates; Shastari-ruler or oppressor.\n\nMeaning of the word:\nMa--while I am here; Shastari-ruler or oppressor. You don't need to be afraid of this lowly caste Shudra.\nYou don't need to be afraid. And, O Go-Pata, as long as I am the ruler or the oppressor of the Khalo,\nyou do not need to cry. You will be well in every way.\nMeaning: The safety of oxen, cows, and all other animals can only be ensured when the governance of the 