          "textEn": "286\nMeaning: When wild animals create havoc in a village or town, the police or other people take action to\nkill them. ©. Similarly, it is the duty of the government to immediately kill all anti-social elements,\nsuch as thieves, dacoits, and murderers. The same punishment should be given to animal abusers as well,\nbecause animals are also the subjects of the state. Praja means, one who is born in the state, and in this,\nhuman and animal both are included. Whichever creature is born in the kingdom, it is his fundamental right that\nhe remains under the protection of the king. The animals of the forest are also the subjects of the king, therefore they also have the right to live.\nTherefore, they also have the right to live. So, what can be said about domestic animals like cows and oxen? If any creature scares another creature, then he is very wicked, and the king should\nimmediately kill such a wretch.\nJust as a wild animal that causes havoc is killed,\nin the same way, the person who unnecessarily kills or harms wild animals or other animals,\nshould be immediately punished. According to divine law, all living beings, no matter what form they are in,\nare children of God, and no one has the right to kill another animal unless the divine\nlaw-code gives its command. A tiger can kill small animals for its livelihood.\nBut a human cannot kill any animal for his livelihood. This is the law of that God,\nwho has made this rule that one animal lives by eating another. Similarly, vegetarians also live by eating other\nliving beings. Therefore, the rule is that whatever is fixed in divine law, according to that, only by eating special living beings should a living being survive. The instruction of Ishopanishad is that\na man should live according to the instructions of God, not according to his own will.\nMan, given by God,\ncan live by eating various kinds of food, fruits, and milk, and, except for a few cases,\nhe does not need to eat animal mea