          "textEn": "299\n\nMeaning of the word:\nTapah--austerity; Shaucham--purity; Daya--compassion; Satyam--truth; Iti--in this way; Pada:--UF; Hel:--in Satyuga; Kritah--\nestablished; Adharma--unrighteousness; Anshaih--by parts; Trayah--the three together; Bhagnah--broken; Smayah--ego; Sajla--excessively\nin the age of truth; your four legs were established by the four rules of penance, purity, compassion, and truth.\n\nIn the age of truth, your four legs were established by the four rules of austerity, purity, compassion, and truth. But it seems that because of the unrighteousness that is pervading everywhere in the form of ego, lust, and intoxication,\nyour three feet have been broken. Meaning: The illusory power or physical nature shows its effect on the living being in the proportion that\nin the proportion in which the living being becomes a victim of the illusory attraction of Maya.\nAttracted by the brilliance of the light,\nthey become victims of fire. Similarly, the power of deceit, Maya, always entertains bound souls\nand keeps throwing them into the fire of delusion. The Vedic scriptures warn bound souls\nthat they should not fall prey to this illusion, but rather get rid of it. The Vedas warn us that we\nshould not go into the darkness of ignorance, but move forward on the path of light. God himself also warns us\nthat conquering the deceptive power of Maya is extremely difficult, but one who completely goes into the refuge of God\ncan easily conquer it. But going to the shelter of the lotus feet of God\nis not easy. Such refuge can only be found by men endowed with penance, purity, compassion, and truth. These four rules of an advanced civilization were noteworthy characteristics of Satyuga. In that era, every human being was, in a way, a highly qualified Brahmin, and in the ashrams, all were Paramhansas, meaning highly qualified ascetics.\nDue to cultural basis, illusion did not deceive humans. Such a strong character person\nwas able to keep himself away from the noose of Maya. But gradually, as the original principles of Brahmin culture\nbecame -asceticism, purity, compassion, and in the truth, the proportionality of ego, lust, and intoxication in\nthe cause of reduction, as the path to salvation or the path of divine bliss became further and further distant from human society,\nit became further and further away. As the duration of Kali Yuga continues to increase, people are becoming extremely arrogant, and they remain attached to women\nand intoxicants. Due to the influence of Kali Yuga, even a poor person is proud of their pennies;\nwives wear attractive clothes to win men's hearts, and men have become addicted to drinking alcohol,\nsmoking, drinking tea, and chewing tobacco. All these habits, or civilization"
