          "textEn": "303\n\nMeaning of the word\nThus--in this way; Dharmam--righteousness; Mahim--to the earth; Cha--also; Ev--in the manner; Sanvativita--giving consolation; Maha-\nRathah--the commander who fought alone against thousands, the great warrior; Nishatam--brightness; Adade--taken; Khadgam--sword; Kalaye--\nTo kill Kali; Adharma--of unrighteousness; Hetave--the root cause \\\n\nThus, Maharaj Parikshit, who could fight alone against a thousand enemies, gave consolation to Dharma and\nEarth. Then, to kill Kali directly because of all the wicked,\nHe took out his sharp sword. Meaning: As has been said above, Kali is that which deliberately, through the scriptures,\ncommits all kinds of sinful deeds. This Kali Yuga will definitely be filled with all the\nactivities of Kali, but it does not mean that all the leaders, administrators,\nscholars, and intelligent people of the society, or above all, the devotees of God, will sit idle and\nremain inactive towards all the activities of Kali.\nIn the rainy season, there is definitely abundant rain, but it does not mean that people should not adopt measures to avoid rain. Of the executive presidents of the state and\nIt is the duty of others that they take necessary action against the activities of Kali or against the men influenced by Kali.\nMaharaj Parikshit is the ideal executive president of the state, because he is immediately ready to kill Kali with his\nSharp sword. Administrators should ensure that they not only\npass anti-corruption resolutions, but they should also be prepared to kill those men who spread corruption with sharpened swords,\nwith the vision of scriptures. By allowing liquor shops,\nthe administrators cannot stop the unrighteous activities. They should ensure that all drug and\nalcohol shops are immediately closed, and those who are addicted to drugs should not hesitate to be given the death penalty.\nThis is the only way to stop the activities of Kali, as has been demonstrated here by\nthe great warrior King Parikshit. Having abandoned the king's garland, which was considered auspicious,\nHe, with his head on the ground, became overwhelmed with fear. 29)."
