          "textEn": "304\n\nMeaning of the word:\nTam- that; Jighansum- desirous of killing; Abhipretya- knowing from afar; Vihayah- leaving; Nrip- laanchanam- of the king;\nVe kavat- foot root at the feet; Shirsa- by the head; Samagat- completely surrendered; Bhay-vimlah-\n\nWhen Kali understood that the king wanted to kill him, he immediately abandoned the king's\ndisguise and, fearing, bowed his head and completely surrendered himself before them. Meaning: The robe of Kali's reign was a fake. A royal attire is suitable for a king or a Kshatriya, but\nwhen a person of lower caste assumes the false guise of a king, then like Maharaj Parikshit,\ntheir true identity is revealed by the challenge of an honest Kshatriya. A true Kshatriya never\nsurrenders himself. He accepts the challenge of his opponent Kshatriya and either\nfights till death or wins. A true Kshatriya would never surrender.\nIn Kali Yuga, there are countless imposters who pretend to be administrators, but their secret\nis revealed when a true Kshatriya challenges them. Therefore, when the disguised Kali saw that\nit was beyond his capacity to fight Maharaj Parikshit, he bowed his head like a subordinate\nand removed his royal attire. Fallen, but at your feet, O mighty-armed one, kind to the helpless. Taking refuge, he laughed, as if to say, 'What is this?' 30. Meaning of the word:\nFallen - fallen; feet - feet; brave - brave; please - out of mercy; humble - merciful; refuge - one who is worthy of refuge; protector of the refugee; not- not; bound - bound; worthy of singing; aha - said; cha - also; idam - this; laughing - laughing; like - like;\n.\n\nThe protector of the surrendered and praiseworthy Maharaj Parikshit in history did not kill that wretched, surrendered, and\nfallen Kali, but instead, he began to laugh with compassion, because he is a compassionate person.\n\nMeaning: When even an ordinary Kshatriya does not kill a surrendered person, then what can be said for Maharaj Parikshit,\nwho is naturally compassionate and kind to the poor. They were laughing because"
