          "textEn": "339\nFrom the pen, they give birth as the first living being in the physical world, not from the womb of Lakshmi Devi, who are constantly engaged in their\nservice. These are some examples of their complete freedom and completeness. They don't have to do anything.\nIt doesn't mean that they are formless. They are divinely, inconceivably, from powers\nSo perfect that just by wishing, everything happens. They don't have to make any physical or\nprivate efforts. Therefore, they are called Yogeshwar, meaning the master of all yogic powers.\nThey are called Yogeshwar.\nHowever, that which is created from the feet,\nFrom the water of the ocean, which is the beginning of the world's destruction.\nThat which is purified by the head, that supreme Mukunda,\nIn what name is the divine substance in the world? 21). Meaning of the word:\nAtha--therefore; Api--even so; Yat--whose; Pada-nakh--toe nails; Avasrishtam--falling out; Jagat--the entire universe; Falta--Brahmaji; Suga--united; Arhaan--worship; Ambha--water; Sa--with; Isha--Lord Shri Krishna; Punati--purified;\nKa--who; Naam--name; Loke--in the world;\nBhagwat--God; Pada--foot, established; Artha--worthy. Brahmaji gathered the water coming out of the nails of his feet to Lord Shivji on his head\nto accept. This water (Ganges) is purifying the entire universe including Shivji\nis making. Meaning: The concept of many gods in Vedic literature is completely misleading by ignorant people.\nGod\nis one and unique, but He expands in many ways, and this is confirmed in the Vedas. God's expansions are countless, but some of them are also living beings. Living beings are not as powerful as the complete parts of God.\nTherefore, there are two types of expansions (parts). Brahmaji is generally\none of the living beings, and Shivji is the medium between God and living beings. In other words,\nBrahmaji and Shivji, who are the foremost among the gods, are not equal to or greater than Lord Vishnu. Lakshmi Devi and powerful deities like Brahma and Shiva, engaged in the worship of Vishnu or Lord Krishna"
