          "textEn": "360\n\nThe protector of dharma, the king, is the emperor, the great-mouthed one.\nI am the witness, the great fortunate one, the king, the best of kings, the best of kings.\nThe one who is always in the hermitage, the one who is hungry, does not deserve our curse. 46. Meaning of the word:\nDharma-pala: Protector of Dharma; Nara-pati: King; Va: He; Tu: But; Samrat: Emperor; Vrihat: Extreme; Shravah: Well-known;\nSakshat: Directly; Maha-bhagavata: Of the highest order of devotees of the Lord; Raja-rishi: Saints among kings; Haya-medhayaat: One who performs the Ashwamedha Yagna; Kshut: Hunger; Truddh: Thirst; Shram-yutah: One who is tired; Dinah: One who is afflicted by misfortune; Na: Never; Eva: Only;\nThus; Asmattah: By us; Shapam: A curse; Ahathi: Deserving of it; Hey: To him; Kshut: Hunger; Truddh: Thirst; Shram-yutah: One who is tired; Dinah: One who is afflicted by misfortune; Na: Never; Eva:.\n\nEmperor Parikshit is a holy king. He is very famous and a great devotee of God.\nHe is a saint among kings and he performed many Ashwamedha Yagyas. When such a king,\nsuffers from hunger and thirst, and is tired, then he is not worthy of being cursed in any way.\nMeaning: After explaining the general code of conduct for the position of king, and after showing that\na king cannot make any mistakes, therefore, he is never condemnable, Muni Shamik was especially wanting to say something about King Parikshit.\nThe specific qualities of Maharaj Parikshit have been briefly given here. If King Parikshit's calculation is done only in the form of a king, then he was a very famous\nruler who ruled according to the religious rules of the king. In the scriptures, all castes and\nthe duties of the ashram have been described. In the Bhagavad Gita (18.42), the qualities of a Kshatriya that are mentioned,\nall of them were present in Emperor Parikshit. He was also a great devotee of God and a person of form.\nOff | It is never appropriate to curse such a king when 