          "textEn": "368\n\nMeaning of the word:\nSah--He/She; Chintayant-- thinking; Ittham-- thus; Ath--now; Ashwrnaut-- heard; Chatha-- in the manner;\nMunih- of the sage; Sut- said by the son; Nirruti: death; Takshak- known as the serpent-bird; Sah- He/She;\nSadhu: good and auspicious; Mene- accepted; Na- not; Chirena- for a long time; Takshak- the serpent;\nAnalam- fire; Prasaktasya- of those who are attached; Virakti- of detachment; Karanam- reason..\n\nWhen the king was repenting in this way, he received news of his imminent death, which was to be caused by a snake-bird bite according to the curse given by the sage's son. The king took it\nas good news, because it would create detachment from worldliness in him. Meaning: True happiness is attained only when the spiritual element or the cycle of birth and death is stopped.\nThe cycle of birth and death can only be broken by returning to the abode of the Lord. Even after attaining the supreme abode in the material world, one cannot escape the cycle of birth and death; yet we do not adopt the path to attain perfection. This path of accomplishment frees a person from all material attachments,\nand in this way, he becomes worthy of entering the spiritual world. Therefore, those who are\npoor are better candidates than prosperous people. Maharaj Parikshit was a great\ndevotee of God and was an authentic candidate to go to the abode of God, but even so, their\nwith the biggest obstacle was their material wealth as the Chakravarti Emperor, due to which they were not able to attain their real position as a companion of God in the divine sky.\nAs a devotee of God, he\ncould understand that although the curse of the Brahmin boy was foolish, it was still a blessing for them\nbecause it was the cause of detachment from both worldliness - political or social. Shamik\nThe sage also, after expressing grief over the incident, considering it his duty, sent this information to the king\nso that he would be prepared to go to the abode of the Lord. Shamik Muni sent a message to the king that although his son Bhangi was a radiant Brahmin boy, but due to being foolish, he misused his spiritual power\nto curse the king in vain. The king putting a snake around the sage's neck was not enough reason to give the curse of death. But since there was no way to take back the curse, therefore the king\nwas informed that he should be prepared for death within a week. Shamik Muni and Raja"
