          "textEn": "369\nBoth were self-evident souls. Shamik Muni was a Yogi, and Maharaj Parikshit was a devotee; therefore, there was no difference between the two\nregarding self-realization. Neither of them was afraid of dying. Maharaj Parikshit should have gone to the sage to apologize, but the king's\ninformation of his imminent death was sent by the sage with so much sorrow that the king now did not want to\nmake the sage more ashamed by his presence. He decided to prepare for his imminent death and to find a way to go to Bhagavad Gita. This human life is an opportunity to return to the abode of God or to escape from the cycle of birth and death, from the ocean of existence.\nIn this way, in the Kanashram-Dharma system, every man and woman is\ntaught for this task. In other words, the Varna-ashrama system is also called Sanatan Dharma. The Varna-Srama system prepares man to return to the world-sustaining order.\nIn this way, a householder\nis ordered to become a forest dweller, go to the forest, attain complete knowledge, and before his inevitable death, to take Sanyas\n(renunciation). O King, Parikshit was fortunate that he received seven days' prior notice before his death.\nBut ordinary people do not get any such definite prior notice,\nalthough death is inevitable for everyone. Foolish people forget this certain fact of death and\nignore their duty to prepare for going to God. They waste their lives in the brutal acts of eating, drinking, and enjoying. Such an irresponsible life is adopted by the people of Kali Yuga,\nbecause they harbor sinful desires for Brahmin culture, divine consciousness, and the despise of cow protection,\nand the state is responsible for this. The state should spend money for the progress of these\nthree things and educate people to prepare for death.\nThe state that does this, is the real welfare state. It would be good if the state of India, the ideal administrator,\nfollows the ideals of Maharaj Parikshit; it should not imitate other materialistic states\nwho do not have any knowledge of the divine abode, which is the ultimate goal of human life. Due to the decline in the ideals of Indian\ncivilization, not only in India but also in foreign countries, there has been a decline in civic life."
