          "textEn": "378\n\nMeaning of the word\nOther--other many; T— also; Devarshi-- sage among the gods; Braharshi-- equal to a sage; Varyaah— best; Rajarshi-Varyaah—\nThe best among the Rajarshis; Arun-Aadi—distinctive position among the Rajarshis; Cha— and; Nana— other many; Arsheya-Pravaraan— among the lineages of the Rishis;\nThe best; Sametanu— together, grouped; Abhyarchya— by worshiping; Raja— the emperor; Shirsa— bowing the head on the ground; Vavande—\nbowed down..\n\nIn addition to these, there were many other Devarshis, kings, and descendants of various sages, specifically kings,\nwho came, who are called Arunadaya. When they all gathered to meet the emperor (Parikshit),\nthe king welcomed everyone appropriately by bowing his head and paying respects.\nHe did so.\nMeaning: The practice of bowing the head to the ground to show respect to elders is an excellent\netiquette, by which guests consider themselves highly respected. Even a high-ranking\noffender is forgiven for doing so, and Maharaj Parikshit was highly respected by all the sages and\nkings. He welcomed all these great men with humility,\nso that they could be forgiven for their offenses. Generally, in the final stage of life, this\nhumble method is followed by every sensitive person, so that before departing, he can be forgiven. In this way, Maharaj Parikshit received the good wishes of the gods to return to the abode of the divine. In happiness, in those, again,\nThe humble obeisance, what is desired by oneself.\nHaving made an announcement, with a clear mind,\nHe appeared before him, holding his hand. 12). Meaning of the word:\nHappiness-- with happiness; Upavisthishu- all seated; Ath- thereafter; Teshu- to them (to the guests); Bhuyah- again; Krita- having bowed; Pranamah- having bowed; Swa- own; Chikirshitam- having requested; Yat- that; Vijnapayam- having announced; As- having presented; Vivikta- having attained; Chetah- having become; Agre- in front of them; Abhigruhit- having accepted;