          "textEn": "32\n\nMeaning: The perfections of Yoga can be attained only through devotional activity. Brahmavad, which is\na method of experiencing the omnipresent presence of the Almighty, is a type of mental training,\nwhich makes one accustomed to devotional belief, and the Yogi is able to achieve such\nsuccessful culmination of yogic efforts through this devotional tendency. But without even a trace of devotion, a person\ndoes not attain such a successful position. From the perspective of Brahmavada, the devotional atmosphere that arises, it further\ndevelops into devotion, and for the non-differentialist, this is the only benefit. Bhagavad Gita\n(12.5) confirms that the unique path to self-realization is more painful, because it\nreaches the goal by wandering, although even the non-uniquists after a long time\nare attracted to the tangible form of God. Rajovach\nAs it is understood, O Brahman, where there is agreement.\nYadashi va haredashu purushasya mano malam. 22). Meaning of the word.\n\nKing said-- The fortunate king said; as-- in the way; Sandharyate-- is assumed; Brahman- He is Brahmin;\nDharana-- assumption; Yatra- where; Tatha- like; Samamta- in summary; Yadrishi- like; Va- or; Hareet- completely destroys;\nAshu- without delay; Purushasya- of a person; Manah- of the mind; Malam- stool..\n\nThe fortunate King Parikshit further asked: O Brahmin, please explain in detail\nwhere and how the mind should be directed? And how can the concept be stabilized so that\nall the dirt of the human mind can be removed? Meaning: The entire impurity of the heart of a bound soul is the root of all its sufferings. A bound soul is surrounded by many sufferings of the world,\nbut due to his complete ignorance, he is unable to remove the dirt accumulated in his heart in this material world during the duration of his long captivity\nof life.\nIn reality, he\nhas come to serve God according to his will, but due to the dirt of the heart, he\nwants to fulfill his own imagined desires. These desires, instead of providing him with peace of mind,\nkeep creating new problems and thus bind him to the cycle of birth and death. Desire"
