          "textEn": "70\nKnowledge has been done, it will leave such unwanted things. When a bound soul, through the practice of knowledge,\nsees that material needs are undesirable, then he breaks ties with them. This state of knowledge is called detachment, that is, detachment from unwanted things. We have already told you that a spiritualist should be self-reliant and should not beg from wealthy blind men to fulfill the eternal\nneeds of his life. Shukdev Goswami has\nsuggested alternatives for such essential needs of life, such as eating, sleeping, and living problems,\nbut he has not suggested any alternative for the satisfaction of lust. One whose inner self\nis still agitated by lust, should not accept the Sanyas Ashram.\nFor one who has not attained this\nstate, the question of taking Sanyas Ashram does not arise. In this way,\nunder the guidance of a true guru, through gradual devotion and by following the principles of the Bhagavat, a person\nbefore taking Sanyas Ashram will be able to control at least the gross lust. Thus, purification (parivarjana) means, in order, to be freed from lust, and its attainment here is by the following method-- beginning from the feet of God, through His meditation-- §. Man\nshould first see how much he has been freed from lust, only then should he try to rise above\n(focusing on) the upper parts of God. The tenth canto of Shrimad Bhagavatam is the\nhumorous face of the Lord. There are many novice learners who immediately start studying from the tenth canto,\nand especially those five chapters in which the description of Lord's Raas Leela is there. This is\ndefinitely inappropriate.\nFrom such inappropriate study or listening to the Bhagwat, material opportunists\nIn the name of Bhagwat, by being engrossed in worldly life, have created chaos. The work of defaming Bhagwat in this way has been accomplished by the actions of so-called devotees. Before someone performs a Bhagwat-vachan (recitation of the Bhagavata),\nhe should be free from all kinds of lust. Shri Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur\nhas explained the meaning of purity as being detached from worldly matters. They say--As the wise purify themselves,\nThey abandon the poisonous attachment. Thus, thus, by the difference in the purification of the mind, the liberation of the mind of meditation is attained. And as soon as someone"
