          "textEn": "79\nHe holds them in his heart and worships them every moment. Meaning: The Bhagavad-gita mentions Sadhāraṇa (my abode) many times, and according to Lord Krishna, the supreme abode is Vaikuntha, which is situated in the sky, or what is called the abode of God. In that space, which is very far from the physical space and its seven coverings, there is neither the need for the sun or the moon,\nnor for electricity for light, because these worlds are self-illuminated and more radiant than physical suns. All devotees of God live in close contact with God, or in other words,\nthey consider God as their only trustworthy friend and well-wisher. They, including Brahma, the lord of the universe,\ndo not care about all the worldly beings. Only they can see all the Vaikuntha realms. Such pure devotees, living under the guidance of God, do not fall into any artificial worries of divine knowledge, nor\ndo they waste time in the explanation of what is Brahma, what is Maya.\nThey neither consider themselves to be false with God,\nnor do they argue that God has a separate existence or that it is eternal.\nNeither is the soul God, or when God incarnates, he assumes a physical body. Nor are they concerned with any obscure philosophical principles, which in reality are many obstacles in the path of divine knowledge. Besides the non-differentialist or non-devotee class, there are also classes who call themselves\ndevotees of God, but by keeping the feeling of liberation hidden in their inner selves, they want to become one with the non-differential Brahman. They find their wrong path of devotion through open indulgence and lead other\nsuch people astray, who are either fools or equally addicted. According to Vishwanath Chakrabarti, all these disgraced and addicted great souls are evil spirits in disguise. Such disgraced and lecherous\nIn this verse, Shukdev Goswami has separated them from the list of yogis.\nThus, Vaikuntha Loka is the actual best possible abode, called Paramapadam. The unique Brahman light is also called the supreme abode, because like the rays of the sun, these are the rays of Vaikuntha Loka. In Bhagavad Gita (14.27), it is clearly stated that the unspecialized Brahman light depends on the body of God.\nAnd since every object directly and indirectly depends on the Brahman light"
