          "textEn": "82\nStanding at the navel, placing the heart on the base, therefore,\nYou are the one who is lost in the ocean of the world.\nThen, with concentration, the wise one,\nPlace the root of your own palm on the base. 20>. Meaning:\nIn the navel; situated; in the heart; placed; from there; with speed; on the chest; after that; to take away; the sage; the meditative devotee; then; to investigate;\nTo search; with intellect; thoughtful; meditative; the lower part of the palate; slowly;\nTo take away; slowly..\nA meditative devotee should take the life air slowly from the navel to the heart, from the heart to the chest,\nand from there to the lower part of the palate. He should find and extract the appropriate places with intelligence. Note: There are six cycles of air movement. A wise devotee should use their intellect and focus their attention\nto discover these places.\nAmong these, the chakra mentioned before is the Swadhishthana chakra or the energy center of life force.\nAbove it, below the navel, is the Manipur chakra. When the upper part of the heart is\ninvestigated, the Anahata chakra is found, and even above that, when the life force is stabilized at the base of the palate,\nthe Vishuddhi chakra is found. Therefore, one should bend their eyebrows.\nWithout expecting the seven-fold body.\nStanding, one should bend halfway,\nWithout disgrace, one should release the head. 29). Meaning of the word:\nTherefore, from there; of the ears; in between; should be taken; restrained; seven-sided;\nVolume: the outlet of the life-air; unasked: independent of all material pleasures; having remained; a moment: a moment's duration; half:\nHalf; unoccupied: back to the abode of God; sight: whose aim is; discerning: different; head: the inter-eye; disperse: one's own\nbody; leave; supreme; gone; gone.\nThereafter, the devotee should bring his life force between both eyebrows, and then\nClose all seven doors of the life force, and make it his goal to return to the abode of God.\nShould be. If he is free from all desires of material enjoyment, then he reaches the Brahmarandhra"
