          "textEn": "90. Vedic knowledge is authentic, and its attainment is not through experimentation, but through the authentic statements of books narrated by authentic individuals\n(authorities). Just by becoming a scholar, one cannot understand Vedic statements; one must go to that true scholar who has acquired Vedic knowledge through a disciple-\ntradition, as clearly stated in the Bhagavad Gita (4.2). God\nKrishna emphatically says that the knowledge-method that has been explained in the Bhagavad Gita was told to the Sun God\nand this knowledge was received by the Sun God from his son Manu through the disciple lineage, and from Manu\nto King Ikshvaku (the grandfather of Lord Ramachandra). Thus, this knowledge system continued to be received by great sages one by one.\nBut in chronological order, this authentic tradition was broken, therefore,\nFor the re-establishment of knowledge, God gave the same knowledge to Arjuna, because being a devotee of God,\nHe was worthy of understanding it.Arjun's understanding of the Bhagavad Gita, its description has also been done (Bhagavad Gita 10.12-13), but there are many foolish people who do not follow Arjun's footsteps to understand the essence of the Bhagavad Gita. They present their interpretations,\nwhich are as foolish as they are and act as obstacles in the path of true knowledge, and\nare misleading to the ignorant or the Shudras. It is said that before understanding the Vedic statements,\na man should become a Brahmin. This accusation is as important as the accusation that\nno lawyer can become a lawyer without graduating. Such an objection is not an obstacle to anyone's progress,\nbut is necessary to understand a particular science well.\nThose who are not qualified Brahmins, they\nmake arbitrary interpretations of Vedic knowledge. A worthy Brahmin is one who, under the guidance of an authentic guru,\nhas received rigorous training. Vedic knowledge helps us in understanding our relationship with Lord Krishna, and to attain the desired fruit of going back to the divine abode, it guides us in performing appropriate actions. But materialists do not understand it. They want to plan to live happily in such a place\nwhere there is no happiness. They are for false pleasure, either through Vedic rituals or through space travel"
