",
          "textEn": "4\nAvoid desires and instead, go back to the divine abode and seek a permanent life.\nOne should perform religious duties, the best of verses, and the duties of ancestors.\nOne should protect, desire the meritorious, and desire the Marutganas. 8. Shabdarman\nDharma-Artha-H: for spiritual growth; Uttam-Shlokam: to the Supreme God or to those who are attached to Him; Tantuh: to the offspring; Tanvann: for their protection; Pitrun: to the inhabitants of the father's world; Yajet: to worship; Raksha-Kamah: for the seeker of protection;\nPunya-Janan: to the holy persons; Ojah-Kamah: to the one who desires strength; Marut-Ganan: to the gods..\nFor the spiritual development of knowledge, a person should worship Lord Vishnu or his devotees,\nand to protect the lineage and for the progress of the clan, he should worship various deities.\nHe should worship.\nMeaning: The path of religion leads to progress on the path of spiritual development, ultimately to Lord Vishnu,\nwith his unique radiance, his omnipresent Supreme Being form, and ultimately to awakening the eternal relationship with his tangible form through spiritual\ndevelopment. The person who wants to establish a good lineage and wants to live happily with the progress of momentary physical relationships,\nshould seek refuge in the ancestors and the gods of other sacred realms. Like this,\ndifferent gods, worshippers of different categories, ultimately reach the worlds of those gods.\nBut one who reaches the Vaikuntha world situated in Brahmajyoti attains the highest perfection. The king should perform the following rituals: he should observe a vow.\nKama-kama-yajet sam-am-kam-am Purusham Param. 9>. Shabdartham\nRajya-Kaamah-- one who desires the kingdom; Manun-- the semi-incarnations of God; Devan-- the gods; Nikratim-- the Asuras; Tu-- but; Abhicharan-- one who desires victory over the enemy; Yajet-- to worship; Kaam-- desiring sensual gratification; Indra- the deity of the moon; Akamah-- desireless; Purusham-- of the Supreme Being; Paramah-- the supreme.\nOne who desires to attain the kingdom should worship the Manus; Somam-- of the lunar deity; Akamah-- desireless; Purusham-- of the Supreme Being; Paramah-- the supreme.\nOne who desires to attain the kingdom should worship the Manus. The person\nWho desires to conquer enemies, to the Asuras and to the one who desires sensual pleasure, to him"
