          "textEn": "42\nThat is definitely a corpse, and the person who has not experienced the fragrance of the basil leaves offered at the feet of God,\nis like a dead body even while breathing. Meaning: According to Srila Viswanatha Chakravarthy, a person who is breathing is a ghost. When a human\ndies, he is called dead, but when he again appears invisible, in a subtle form, from our present sight,\nand acts, then such a dead body is called a ghost. Ghosts are always bad elements,\nwhich create terrible situations for others. Similarly, ghost-like devotees, who have neither any respect for pure devotees,\nnor for the Vishnu Shrivigrahs of temples, they always create a terrible\nsituation for the devotees. God does not accept offerings from impure spirits. A common\nsaying is that before showing affection towards a beloved, a man has to love the beloved's dog.\nIs.\nThe state of pure devotion can only be attained through the sincere service of a pure devotee of God.\nTherefore, the first condition of devotion to God is to be a servant of a pure devotee, and the fulfillment of this condition is through this statement:\n'*To wear the dust of the feet of that pure devotee who has served another pure devotee.'\nIs.\"This is the pure disciple tradition or the devotion tradition. When Maharaj Rahugan asked the supreme saint Jadbharat how he attained the state of a Paramahansa,\nhe gave the following answer (Bhagwat 5. 12. 12).\n\nRahuganaitattapasa na yati na chejjaya nirvapad guhadva. Without water, the great offering of water is not performed. “\"Hail, King Rahugana! Without purifying the entire body with the dust of the lotus feet of great devotees,\none cannot attain the complete state of devotion or the supreme state of life, the state of Paramahansa.\nThis penance cannot be attained by Vedic\nworship rituals, taking Sanyas (renunciation), performing duties of a householder, chanting Vedic Wis, or in hot sun or\nunderwater or in front of a burning fire.In other words, Lord Krishna is the property of his pure, liberated devotees; therefore, only a devotee can entrust Krishna to another devotee; Krishna is never obtained directly. Therefore"
