          "textEn": "43\nBhagavan Chaitanya himself said to himself, \"Gopadhartha, Fadakamalayodasidasataradasaha.\" \"* That is, in Vrindavan, the servants of the Lord who take care of the Gopa Balas,\nare extremely obedient servants.\" A pure devotee never reaches God directly, but he tries to please the servant of God's servants, and\nin this way, God becomes pleased. Only then can one taste the basil leaves offered at the lotus feet of the devotee God.\nIt is said in the Brahma Samhita that one does not attain God by becoming a great scholar of Vedic literature,\nbut rather, they are easily attained through their pure devotee. All the people of Vrindavan\npray for the grace of Shri Radha Rani, the divine power in the heart of Lord Krishna, who is the supreme complete\nSukumar Hriday Ardhadarini, who is in accordance with the Siddhastha state of the feminine nature of the world.\nTherefore,\ndevout devotees easily attain the grace and simplicity of Radharani, and once they approve of such a devotee of Lord Krishna,\nGod immediately makes him his companion. Therefore,\nthe conclusion is that instead of directly seeking God's grace, one should seek the grace of a devotee. By doing this (through the grace of a devotee), the natural attraction to serve God will be awakened again. Tadashum-saram hridayam batedam\nYad grihyamtai harinam-dheya-hai. When there is no change, when there is no distortion,\nIn the eyes, in the water, in the body, there is joy. 24). Meaning of the word:\nTat-that; Ashm-Saram--made of steel; Hridayam--heart; Bat-Idam--certainly this; Yat-that; Grihyamanaih--to be accepted; Harinam--the holy name of God; Dheyai:--with concentration of mind; Na-not; Vikriyet-changed; Ath-in this way;\nYada-when; Vikarah-reaction; Netre-in the eyes; Jalam-tear; Gatra-in the body; Ruheshu-in the pores; Harshah-the effulgence of joy; L-laughter\nIndeed, that heart is made of steel, which, even when concentrated, does not change upon uttering the holy name of God; 