          "textEn": "89\nCan have a vision of God through the medium (Anupashyanti) | A pure devotee, through mental contemplation,\nnever tries to have a vision of God, but he follows the footsteps of the teachers.\n(WETS Yen Gathah Sapanthah). Therefore, there is no difference between Vaishnava teachers and God and the devotees.\nThere is no difference. Bhagavan Chaitanya emphatically states that the soul is eternally subservient to God, and it is one and the same\nat the same time, separate from God. This principle of Bhagavan Chaitanya is equally accepted by all four sects of the Vaishnava faith.\nEven after liberation, all accept the eternal servitude of God. And there is no valid Vaishnava Acharya who can think of God and himself as one.\nDevotees who are 100% engaged in the service of God, this humility bestows the state of Samadhi on the devotee of God.\nThis enables him to experience every object, because God appears before the devoted devotee.\nAs in the Bhagavad Gita (10.10) It is said. God, being the master of every individual's intellect,\nprovides appropriate intellect to his devotee, so that\nthe devotee becomes aware of the real facts about God and his various powers.\nGod neither manifests from anyone's power of contemplation, nor from the net of words concerning the ultimate truth. They appear before the devotee\nOnly when they are fully satisfied with the devotee's tendency of service. Shukadeva\nGoswami is neither a thinker, nor is he a supporter of the principle that 'as many paths, so many opinions'. But they only\npray, invoking their divine bliss from God. © | This is the only way to know God.\nPrachodita yena pura saraswati\nvitvanvatajasya satim smritim hadi |\nswa-lakshana praadurbhavat kilasyaatha\nsas me rishinamrupbhah prasidatam. 22). Meaning of the word:\nAwakened, beloved; by whom--by whom; at the beginning of creation; Saraswati--goddess of knowledge; expanded--extended; unborn--\nOf the first living being, Brahma; Sati--memory-powerful; in the heart--in the heart; self--own; characteristic--marked; arose--was produced; as if--as if; from the beginning--from the beginning; Rishis--teachers; Rishabha--the foremost;\nBe pleased--be pleased."
