          "textEn": "]9\nHe inspired them to attain such divine knowledge, because this knowledge is directly descended from God. Therefore, the Vedas are called apaurusheya, that is, not given by any created being. Before creation, God was present (Narayana: Para Avyaktaat), therefore, the words spoken by God are\nwaves of divine sound. There is a very big difference between the two types of sound, natural and unnatural. The Bhautik-shakhi can only consider the sound or the sound vibrating in the physical sky,\nTherefore, we should know that the Vedic sounds inscribed in the form of symbolic expressions cannot be understood by anyone in this\nuniverse until they are inspired by the vibrations of unmanifest sound, which is received from God to Brahma, from Brahma to Narada, from Narada to\nVyasa, and further in this order.\nNo worldly scholar can reveal the true essence of Vedic mantras, nor can they translate them. They cannot be understood until they are inspired or initiated by a valid guru. The first Guru is God himself, and\nThis knowledge has been passed down through tradition, as clearly stated in the fourth chapter of the Bhagavad Gita. Therefore,\ndivine knowledge, until it is obtained from a valid tradition, should be considered as unsuccessful (vipala matah).\nEven if it progresses in art or science to any extent,\nShukdev Goswami, inspired by God in his heart, is praying to God\nso that he can correctly explain the facts related to creation asked by Maharaj Parikshit. A guru is worldly,\nnot like a scholar, a theoretical thinker, but he is a Shrotriya, devoted to Brahman.\nThe greatness of the elements, these are the all-pervading ones.\nHe who creates the earth, the water, the fire, and the sky.\nHe who is the sixteenfold, the complete.\nHe who is adorned, O Lord, with my words. 23). Meaning of the word\nBy the elements; Mahadhih-- of the physical creation; Yah- who; Imah- these all; Purah- body; Vibhuh- of God; Nirmanaya-\nTo create; Shete- lying down;