          "textEn": "94\nThe nectar dripping from her mouth goes into the mouths of her various lovers, which makes them know fine arts like music, dance,\ndecoration, adornment, and all those things that are delicious to God. Such music, dance, and adornment enjoyed by God are not worldly, because God is addressed as the ultimate, that is, divine, from the very beginning. This divine knowledge is unknown to the forgetful bound beings. Lord Vyasadeva, the incarnation of God, compiled the Vedic literature for this reason, so that\na bound soul can remember their lost relationships with God again, therefore man\nshould listen from the lotus-faced Vyasadeva or Shukadeva to understand the nectar donation made by God to his lovers. Through the gradual\ndevelopment of divine knowledge, a person can attain that divine art of music and dance, which God\ndisplayed during his Rasleela.\nBut due to the lack of Vedic knowledge, it is difficult to understand the divine nature of God's Raas-dance and\nmusic. But the pure devotees of God, this nectar,\nenjoy it as profound philosophical discussions and as a kiss from God in the Raas dance,\nbecause there is no physical difference between these two. Etadevatma-bhoo rajan narada viprichhate.\nVeda-garbho abhyadhat sakshat yadaah hariratmanah. 25. Shabdartham.\n'Etat--in this matter; eva--in the same way; atmanah--of the self; bho--from; prathama--first; janma (Brahmaji); rajan--O King; narada--from Narada Muni;\nviprchyate--being asked; veda-garbhaah--being endowed with Vedic knowledge from birth; vyakta--being directly; yat--that;\nAah--what he said; harih--Lord; atmanah--of oneself( from Brahma ) +. Hey Rajan, when asked by Narad, the first-born Brahmaji said the exact same thing in this matter.\nThat which Lord had directly said to his son (Brahma), who was from birth endowed with Vedic knowledge.\nMeaning: As soon as Brahma was born from the navel lotus of Vishnu, he was endowed with Vedic knowledge,\nTherefore, he is called Ved