          "textEn": "23\nThe direction remains. From the supreme intelligent being Brahma to a tiny ant, the intelligence of all beings\nis seen by God as a witness to all actions. The experience of the subtle presence of God\nis felt by that wise person who can study the psychological effects of thinking, experiencing, and desiring. Sattva, Rajas, Tamas, thus are the three Gunas of the non-separative (Godless) nature. The state, the cause, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the cessation, the means, the c 18>. Meaning of the word:\n\nSattva - Satvaguna (quality of goodness); Rajo (quality of passion); Tamo (quality of ignorance); Tamoguna (quality of ignorance) - these are all; Nirgunasya Brahma ke (of the formless Brahman); Gunah (of the three qualities); Trayah (of the three qualities); Sthiti (of the state of being);\nPalan (of the state of being); Sagam (of the state of being); Utpatti (of the state of being); Nirodheshu (in the destruction); Grihitah (accepted); Mayaya (by Maya - the illusionary power); Vibhohi (of the Supreme Lord) - of the Supreme Lord.\n\nThe Supreme Lord is beyond all material qualities in His pure spiritual form, yet for the creation, sustenance, and destruction of the material world, He accepts the qualities of nature through His external power.\nMeaning: God is the master of that external power, which is expressed by the three qualities of Sattva, Rajo, and Tamo--\nand being the master of this power, he is never affected by this attractive power.\nBut the living being remains either affected by such qualities or there remains a possibility of them being affected--\nthis is the main difference between God and the living being. Although the living being is fundamentally one from the point of view of qualitative nature with God,\nthe living being keeps being affected by these qualities. In other words, these physical qualities of God,\nbecause they originate from the external power, are certainly related to God, but this relationship\nis like that of a master and a servant. God is the regulator of physical power, but all living beings are\ntrapped in the physical world, so they are neither masters nor regulators, but they are controlled by such power or\nbecome subject to it.\nIn reality, God, through his inner self or spiritual power, remains eternally manifest in the same way,\njust as the sun and its rays appear in a clear sky. But\nsometimes they generate physical power, just as the sun creates clouds in the clear sky.\nIs. Just as the sun remains unaffected by a piece of cloud, similarly, the infinite God is the embodiment of material power"
