          "textEn": "\nBut; from the form--by transformation; from the water--of the water; from the fragrance--of the fragrance; from the form--from the form; from the preceding--from the preceding; from the connection--from the connection;\nFrom the tradition; Rasa--Rasa; Touch--Touch; Sound--Sound; Form--Qualitatively..\n\nSince the sky is transformed, therefore, air is produced with touch qualities, and according to the previous tradition, the word air and the basic elements of age, that is, touch, mental power, and\nphysical strength, are also complete. When air is transformed with time and nature,\nthen fire is produced, and it takes the form of touch and sound. Because fire also\nis transformed, therefore water appears, which is filled with juice and taste. According to tradition, this is also full of form, touch and sound. And when this water transforms into its\nnanarupa (non-physical form) in the earth, then it appears fragrant, and\naccording to tradition, it is filled with the qualities of rasa (essence), sparsha (touch), shabda (sound), and roopa (form).\nMeaning: The entire creation's process is a gradual evolutionary work, and the development from one element to another\nis what gives diversity to the earth, in which so many trees, plants, mountains, rivers, reptiles,\nbirds, animals, and various kinds of humans are found. The quality of sensory perception is also developing, that is, it\nis produced by sound, then touch, and from touch, form is created, hey. Sky, air, fire, water, and earth\nAlong with the gradual development, taste and smell also arise. They are mutually\ncause and effect, but the original cause is directly God in the form of a part, who is the cause-water-filled Mahavishnu of the great element. Therefore, in the Brahma Samhita, Lord Krishna is described as the cause of all causes.\nAnd in the Bhagavad Gita (10.8), its confirmation is as follows:\n\nI am the origin of all, and all things are from Me."
