          "textEn": "248\nIt is possible, all are included--they are all covered by the immense form of God in all times--past, present, and future--though they remain beyond all of these and always remain in the form of a childish shape. Meaning: God, through his partial expression, is not more than nine inches in the form of the Supreme Being. They expand in a huge form from their yogamaya, in which various forms of the moving and non-moving beings,\nexpressing biological and non-biological all things, come into being. Therefore, the manifestations of the universe\nare not different from God, just as golden ornaments of various forms and colors are not different from the original\nquantity of gold. In other words, God is that supreme being who, within creation, controls every object,\nand yet remains a distinct supreme being, separate from all the manifested physical creation. Hence, he is called Yogesar in Bhagvat Gita (9.4–5).\nEvery object is dependent on the power of Lord Krishna.\nYet, they remain separate and beyond such forms. Even in the Vedic Purusha Sukta of the Rig Mantra,\nits confirmation has been given. At the same time, this philosophical truth of unity and glory was propounded by Lord Shri\nChaitanya Mahaprabhu, and this is called the inseparable principle of non-difference. Brahma, Narada, and all others\nAt the same time, with God, one and separate from Him | We are all with Him in the same way as\nGolden ornaments are like a heap of gold, but no single ornament of gold is equal to a heap of\ngold. This heap of gold never ends, even if countless ornaments are made from it,\nwhy not, Because this is a complete sum; if the complete sum is subtracted from the complete sum, then also the supreme\ncomplete, the complete remains. This fact is inconceivable by our present incomplete senses.\nTherefore,\nLord Chaitanya calls his philosophical principle Achintya, and as it is also confirmed by the Bhagavad Gita and the Bhagavata,\nLord Chaitanya's Achintya Bhedabheda Tattva is a complete vision regarding the ultimate truth. Self-sustaining, the life force shines within and without.\nThus, the self-existent, the self-sustaining, and the self-controlled. 97. Shabdharam"
