          "textEn": "260\nThe argument of the Nirvisheshvadis is that when every object is nothing but God, then\nwhat is the benefit of worshiping God? But the virtuous one worships God with great gratitude,\nusing the material produced from the physical parts of God. Fruits and flowers are obtained from the earth's\nbody, yet the intelligent devotee worships Mother Earth with the material produced from the earth itself.\nThey do. Similarly, the worship of Mother Ganga is done with Ganga water, yet the worshiper gets to enjoy the fruits of such worship. Even if God is worshiped with the material produced from the physical parts of God,\neven then, the worshiper, who is a part of God himself, attains the fruit of devotion to God. On one hand,\nwhere the non-dualist erroneously reaches the conclusion that he is the direct God, on the other hand, the qualified one\nworships God with gratitude and keeps knowing that nothing is different from God.\nTherefore, a devotee wants to put every object in the service of God, because he knows\nthat every object is the property of God, no one can call it their own. This complete\nunderstanding of the one is helpful for the worshiper to remain absorbed in the love and devotion of God, but the one who is without distinction remains always a non-devotee,\nand God never grants him recognition.\nTesu Yagyasya Pavaha Savanaspataha Kushaha.\nThis divine sacrifice is devoid of time and possesses qualities. 24). Meaning of the word\nTesu--such yagyos me; yagyasya- of the completion of the yagya; pavah- animals or materials for the yagya; sa-vanaspataha- with fruits and flowers;\nHIM:—kushah; idam- all these; cha- also; deva- yajanam- the altar of the yagya; kaal- time; upuyakta- time; T- also; 36- excessive;\nGuna- anvitah- appropriate;\nTo complete the yagyotsavas, along with flowers, leaves, and other yagya materials,\nyanj- altar and suitable time (spring) is also required.\nVastunyushodhayah, with the nectar of ointment, iron, and water.\nIn the Vedas, in the Yajus, in the Saman, in the Chatur-hotra, and in the Sattama. 25. Meaning of the word:\nVastuni-- vessels; Oshadhayah-- food; Snehah-- ghee; Ras-loh-mridah-- honey, gold, and clay; Jalam-- water; Rig-cha-- Rigveda;\nYajunshi-- Yajurveda; Samani-- Samaveda; Chatuh-- Hota-ram-- four persons who perform the Yagya; Cha-- all these; Satam-- the most sacred."
