          "textEn": "35\n\nFor such a great man, even the empire of heaven won by his might was of no importance. Meaning: By sacrificing so much material in return for Bali Maharaj, he attained a place in Vaikuntha Loka by receiving the divine grace of God, where he had as much or even more facilities available for eternal enjoyment. Therefore, by renouncing the kingdom of heaven won by force, he\ndid not lose anything. In other words, when God, by snatching away the hard-earned wealth of a crow, in return, bestows his divine devotion, bliss, and knowledge for eternal\nlife, then it should be understood as the special grace of God on such a pure devotee. Material possessions, no matter how attractive they may be, can never be permanent. Therefore, a person\nshould voluntarily renounce such possessions, otherwise, while renouncing this body,\none has to give up all this wealth.\nThe wise man knows that all material possessions are perishable, and the best use of them is to dedicate them to the service of God, so that\nGod may be pleased and grant them a permanent abode in His Supreme Abode.\n\nIn the Bhagavad Gita (15.5-6), the Supreme Abode is explained by God as follows--\n\nThose who are free from attachment, delusion, and faults, and who are content with the fruits of their own actions,\n\n...... That which is not reflected in the sun, nor the moon, nor the fire,\nThat state, which, having gone, does not return, is the supreme abode of mine. The man who has more than what is needed in this world, in the form of a house, land, children, society, friendship, and property,\nall that remains with him for a short time. No one\nCan permanently keep this illusory material emanated by Maya. Such a master remains excessively fascinated about self-\nrealization, therefore, a person either has very little or\nnothing so that he can stay away from the arrogant prestige.\nWe remain tainted in this world due to contact with the three qualities. Therefore, whoever, as much as,