          "textEn": "36\nHe who makes spiritual progress remains free from that much material attachment. ©\nTo attain this state of life, a person should have complete faith in his spiritual existence and its permanent effects. To gain the right knowledge about the stability of self-existence,\none should keep it voluntarily or to fulfill the need of livelihood. Humans should\nbe content with the minimum, without creating artificial needs. Artificial needs are the functions of the senses. The progress of modern civilization is based on the functions of these senses, or in other words,\nIn other words, this civilization is a civilization of sensory gratification. The whole civilization is civilization of soul. The one who desires sensual gratification is like an animal, because animals cannot rise above the functions of their senses. The mind is above the senses.\nThe civilization of mental contemplation is also not complete, because intellect is above the mind.\nAnd the Bhagavad Gita gives us information about intellectual civilization. In Vedic literature, human civilization\nhas been described in many directions, which includes the civilizations of the senses, mind, intellect, and soul. In the Bhagavad Gita, the description of human intellect is found, by which he progresses from the civilization of the soul\nto the front. The Shrimad Bhagavatam is a complete human civilization in the subject matter connected with the soul. Like,\nHe, the human soul, rises to the level of civilization, he becomes worthy of going to the kingdom of God,\nwhich is described in the above verses of the Bhagavad Gita. From the initial information about the kingdom of God, we come to know that there, there is no need for the sun, moon, or electricity.\nBut in the dark physical world, they are needed. Secondly,\nthat whoever reaches that kingdom through the method of the soul's civilization, that is, Bhakti Yoga,\nreaches the supreme accomplishment of life.\nThen the human being becomes 