          "textEn": "333\n\nIn reality, if someone remains engaged in the service of God, then there is no\nneed to sacrifice to the gods. In Vedic literature, the arrangement of yagyas to keep the deities satisfied, that\nis a kind of inspiration for the priest, so that he can realize the presence of higher authorities\ncan. For the control of physical actions by Shri Bhagavan, deities are appointed, and\nAccording to the Bhagavad Gita, if someone worships a deity, it is considered an indirect method of worshiping God.\nIt is considered. But when someone directly worships God, then for him, there is no need to worship deities or\nto perform sacrifices in special circumstances. Therefore, Lord Shri Krishna advised the residents of Vraja\nthat they should not offer any kind of sacrifice to Indra, the king of heaven. But without knowing\nthat Lord Krishna is in Brajbhumi, Indra became very angry with the residents of Braj and he wanted to\ntake revenge for this offense.\nBut being God, Shri Krishna saved the inhabitants of Vraja land and the animals\nfrom his inner power and proved that if someone becomes a devotee of God\nand serves him, then he does not need to please other gods, whether he is\na high-ranking god like Brahma or Shiva. Thus, it has been clearly proven by this incident that Shri Krishna is Shri Bhagwan, and he lives in every situation in the same way, whether in the lap of his mother,\nas a baby, or as a seven-year-old child, or as a 125-year-old old man. But in every\nstate, they never reached the level of a normal human being, even in old age they seemed to be sixteen\nyear old youths. These are some of the unique features of the Divine Body of Christ.\nPlaying in the forest, night, moon, and fire,\nTowards the king, with a faint face,\nIncited by the remembrance of past deeds,\nHe will shower the wealth of the Brajas. 33. Meaning of the word:\n\nKridan- playing; Vane- in the forest of Vrindavan; Nishi- at night; Nishakar Chandrama- moonless night; 