          "textEn": "352\n\nMeaning: Since generally we have the experience of trying-bound living beings to attain the perishable physical world and the physical world, therefore Brahmaji told Naraddev that this impermanent\nworld is the miracle of the outward power of God, and here the struggling bound living beings are the\nstable power of God. The only reason for these transient activities is God, that is, Hari, who is the cause of all\ncauses. But this should not be interpreted to mean that God is everywhere in a formless form. They are separate from the interaction of all external and internal forces. The Bhagavad Gita (9.4) asserts that God is everywhere because He is omnipresent. That which\nis in any manifest form, depends on their power, but being the complete Supreme God, they\nremain detached from every object. Shakti and Shaktiman are one and the same, but they are separated from each other.\nGod cannot be called good or bad for the creation of this sorrowful world, just as\na king cannot be blamed for making a prison in the kingdom. Those who violate the orders of the state,\nFor them, the number of prisons is mandatory. Similarly, God has created this sorrowful\nmaterial world for those who have forgotten Him and are trying to establish dominion over this false world. Even then, they remain eager to take the fallen souls back to their abode,\nand for this, as authentic edema, representatives, and incarnations,\nthey keep providing many opportunities to the bound souls. Because they have no direct\nconnection with this physical world, therefore, they cannot be blamed for its creation. Idam bhagavatam naam yanme bhagavatoditam.\nO Lord, you are the one who is the essence of all the Vibhuti. 51). Meaning of the word:\n\nIdam-- This; Bhagavatam-- Philosophy; Naam-- Named; Yat-- That; Me-- To me; Bhagavatam-- From Lord Bhagavan; Uditam-- Illuminated;\nSangrahah-- Collection; Ayam-- That one; Vibhutinam-- Of various powers; Tvam-- You; Etat-- This philosophy; Vipuli-- Extensive;\nThe complete Supreme Lord himself had told me this philosophy of the essence in a concise form, and this is a collection of his various powers. Now you yourself of this knowledge"
