          "textEn": "378\nhas been explained, Mahabharata is the history of ancient India and the Puranas are also like that. The mention of virtuous deeds\nhas been made in the Upavedas (Smritis), and in these, works like digging ponds and wells are included to provide water to the general public. Planting trees along the roads, building temples and places of worship for God, opening charity centers to give food to the poor, and other similar works\nare called fulfillment. Similarly, the king expressed curiosity to know about the method of fulfilling natural desires for the benefit and satisfaction of all.\nThis is the creation of the self, and also the possibility of the self's liberation.\nAnd also the arrangement for the liberation of the self, in its own form. 22). Meaning of the word:\n\nYah--who is all; Va--or else; Anushayinam--involved in the body of God; Sarga--creation; Pashandasya--of the heretics; Cha--of\nand; Sambhavah--manifestation; Atmanah--of the living beings; Bandh--bondage; Mokshau-- liberated; Cha--also; Vyavasthaanam--being situated; Swa-\nRupatmak Mukta Avastha HL\n\nPlease also explain how the living beings that are contained in the body of God are produced\nand how the heretics appear in this world? Also tell how unborn\nsouls remain present 2\n\nMeaning: A progressive devotee of God should ask an authentic guru how the souls contained in the body of God return at the time of creation. Souls are of two types\n--Nityamukta Jiva and Nityabaddha Jiva. There are also two types of bound beings--obedient and\ndisobedient. The obedient being has two more types--devotee and thinker.\nThe thinkers want identification with the body of God, while the devotees want to remain constantly engaged in the service of God while maintaining their separate existence. Such devotees who are not completely pure and the knowledge-oriented philosophers become bound again in the next creation for further purification. Such bound souls, with progress in devotion to God,\nbecome liberated. Maharaj Parikshit asked all these questions to his guru so that he could become proficient in the knowledge of truth."
